 "what": "Bisect of encode of floats, bench/runtime.cpp arm S, doc bench/docs/floats.cbor, between 2d450a7 and main 2b682f5. One binary per commit that changes include/ (1ae7394 changes nothing in include/ and is skipped). 2d450a7b and 2b682f5b are second builds of 2d450a7 and 2b682f5 for an A/A check. ns per iteration.",
 "why": "Encode of floats was reported about 9 percent slower at main than at 2d450a7.",
 "how": "Google Benchmark, --benchmark_min_time=0.2s, GLIBC_TUNABLES trim_threshold=1073741824, mmap_threshold=33554432, top_pad=268435456. Run 1: 15 binaries, 5 rounds, all binaries alternate in each round, 26.3 s. Run 2: 6 binaries, 12 rounds, alternate, 25.1 s. Each run stays under 30 s.",
 "finding": "edbd393 makes encode of floats slower: +6.5 percent in run 1 and +11.2 percent in run 2 against 2d450a7, while bf0af73 is at -3.4 and -0.8 percent. In edbd393, encoder::float_encode jumps to an out-of-line encoder::head_encode(major, info, argument), which computes the argument width from info at run time (shlx, two cmov, a variable shift) and writes 8 bytes. Before, each of the three float cases wrote its initial byte and a 2, 4 or 8 byte movbe with a constant size, inline. 2b682f5 restores the inline writer, and main is within the A/A difference of 2d450a7 (-1.2 percent in run 2). The 9 percent slowdown at main is not reproduced in this session.",
